{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148073",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $S$ be the event that an apple is sold to the supermarket, $W$ be the event that the apple weights more than $200\\,g,$ and ${V}_{1},{V}_{2},{V}_{2}$ be the events that an apple is of variety $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({V}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{V}_{1})=0.45 \\\\  & P({V}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(W|{V}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({V}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(W|{V}_{3})=0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${V}_{1}⊔{V}_{2}⊔{V}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({V}_{1})+P({V}_{2})+P({V}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $75%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=0.75.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weigh more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket, which is represented as $P(S|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|W) & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W|{V}_{1})P({V}_{1})+P(W|{V}_{2})P({V}_{2})+P(W|{V}_{3})P({V}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.75)}{(0.3)(0.45)+(0.2)(0.15)+(0.5)(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.215} \\\\  & ≈0.698,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
